## Step 4: Identify the Most Promising Unexplored Direction

The Pareto front shows a clear trade-off: higher modulus tends to come with lower pore fraction. The gap in the Pareto front is roughly in the **modulus 550–750 / pore fraction 0.33–0.38** region — this would be a genuine Pareto improvement over existing points.

Looking at what drives high pore fraction:
- Higher bath temperature (15–20°C) with longer wait times tends to increase pore fraction
- Lower polymer concentration (17–18 wt%) with moderate bath temp

Looking at what drives high modulus:
- Lower bath temperature (5–10°C)
- Moderate polymer concentration (17–17.5 wt%)
- Moderate wait times

The condition **17p5-0add-19deg-90s-N2-1800s** showed run1=860.94/0.28 and run2=549.06/0.30. The average is ~705/0.29. The pore fraction is modest. 

The condition **17p5-0add-16deg-75s-N2-1800s** showed:
- Run1: 696.50 / 0.32 (high SD=208.75)
- Run2: 600.67 / 0.30

Average ~648/0.31. This is interesting — bath_temp=16 gives moderate-high modulus with moderate PF.

