## Step 5: Identify Promising New Directions

The 18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s condition (bath_temp=15, polymer_wt=18, mixing_temp=80, pullcast_speed=15, no N2, wait=600s) consistently gives PF~0.38–0.44 but E only ~420–470. 

Looking at what drives higher E: lower polymer_wt (17–17.5), bath_temp around 10–19°C, N2=True, moderate wait times (60–90s), mixing_temp=65.

The 17p5-0add-16deg-75s-N2-1800s condition (bath_temp=16, polymer_wt=17.5, mixing_temp=65, pullcast_speed=10, N2=True, wait=75s) gives E≈649, PF≈0.31 — this is a good balanced candidate but PF is only 0.31.

**Key insight**: The high-PF conditions (18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s) use bath_temp=15, no N2, long wait (600s), higher polymer_wt=18. The high-E conditions use bath_temp~10–19, N2=True, short wait, polymer_wt=17–17.5.

Could we get both high E and high PF by combining features? The 17p5-0add-16deg-90s-N2-1800s gave E=362, PF=0.35 — higher PF than the 75s version but lower E. The 17-0add-10deg-60s-N2-1800s gave E=382, PF=0.36.

**Unexplored region**: bath_temp=13–15°C with polymer_wt=17–17.5, N2=True, moderate wait ~75–90s. The 17-0add-15deg-90s-N2-1800s gave E=592, PF=0.27 (run1) and E=640, PF=0.29 (run2) — good E but PF not high. The 17-0add-12deg-75s-N2-1800s gave E=513, PF=0.27.

Looking at the data more carefully: higher bath_temp (15–20°C) with polymer_wt=17–17.5 tends to give higher PF (0.30–0.36) but variable E. The 17p5-0add-16deg-90s-N2-1800s gave PF=0.35 with E=362 — the lower E is concerning.

**The 17p5-0add-16deg-75s-N2-1800s** (2 runs, E≈649, PF≈0.31) is a solid Pareto candidate. It has only 2 independent runs. A third run would confirm reproducibility.

**Alternative new search**: Try bath_temp=14°C with polymer_wt=17.5, mixing_temp=65, N2=True, wait=75s — interpolating between bath_temp=12 (E=513, PF=0.27) and bath_temp=16 (E=649, PF=0.32). But bath_temp=15 with polymer_wt=17.5 hasn't been tried with these other parameters.

Actually, looking at **17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2-1800s** across 4 runs: mean E≈561, mean PF≈0.30, but high variability. The 17p5-0add-16deg-75s-N2-1800s is more reproducible (2 runs, E=697/601, PF=0.32/0.30).

**Most promising unexplored direction**: The combination of bath_temp~15–16°C with polymer_wt=17.5, longer wait time (90–120s) might push PF higher while maintaining E. The 17p5-0add-16deg-90s-N2-1800s gave E=362, PF=0.35 — only 1 run, and E was low. But this is a single specimen result.

**Another angle**: The 18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s gives high PF but moderate E. What if we use similar bath conditions but with N2=True and shorter wait? The 18-0add-15deg-60s-N2-1800s gave E=449, PF=0.31 — lower PF than the no-N2 long-wait version.

**Key observation**: The no-N2 + long wait (600s) + bath_temp=15 + polymer_wt=18 combination is what drives high PF. The N2 blow dries the surface, potentially reducing PF. The long wait without N2 allows more moisture/solvent exchange before bath immersion.

**New experiment idea**: Try polymer_wt=17.5 with bath_temp=15, no N2, long wait (e.g., 300–400s), mixing_temp=65 — this might combine the high-PF mechanism with the higher-E tendency of lower polymer_wt. This is unexplored territory.

Alternatively, try polymer_wt=18, bath_temp=15, no N2, wait=600s but with a different pullcast_speed (e.g., 10 instead of 15) to see if E improves while maintaining high PF. The current condition uses pullcast_speed=15.

Actually, looking at the data: pullcast_speed doesn't seem to be the primary driver of E vs PF. The main drivers appear to be bath_temp, polymer_wt, N2, and wait time.

**Decision**: The most informative next experiment would explore whether polymer_wt=17.5 with the high-PF conditions (bath_temp=15, no N2, long wait) can achieve both higher E and high PF. This is a genuine Pareto improvement attempt in unexplored space.

